      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The elusive, ghostly failures - a classic case of the \"Goblin in the Machine\" phenomenon. I'm happy to help you tackle this enigmatic issue. Here's a professional root cause analysis (RCA) framework to help you isolate the culprit:\n\n**RCA Framework:**\n\n1. **Gather Data**: Collect as much data as possible about the failures, including:\n\t* Build logs and timestamps\n\t* Test results and error messages\n\t* System resource utilization (CPU, memory, disk I/O)\n\t* Network traffic and latency\n\t* Jenkins configuration and environment variables\n2. **Analyze Patterns**: Look for patterns in the failures, such as:\n\t* Time of day or day of the week\n\t* Build frequency and duration\n\t* Test suite or specific tests that fail\n\t* System resource utilization during failures\n3. **Identify Potential Causes**:\n\t* Environmental resource contention: Is the Jenkins server experiencing high CPU or memory usage during builds? Are there other resource-intensive processes running concurrently?\n\t* Network jitter: Are there any network issues or latency spikes during builds?\n\t* Disk I/O contention: Are there other disk-intensive processes running concurrently, or is the disk I/O pattern",
      "source": "target",
